# =============================================================================
# Secrets. Copy this file to .env and fill in what you need:
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# .env is gitignored -- never commit real credentials. It is also the one file
# an AI assistant is blocked from reading or editing; see the `deny` rules in
# .claude/settings.json.
#
# EVERY NON-SECRET SETTING IS IN `.env.defaults` INSTEAD -- committed, and safe
# to edit. Look there for the schema name, the LLM provider, the region, and the
# quota caps. `.env` is read first and wins, so you can also put a one-machine
# override of any of those keys here.
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Provider credentials -- fill in ONLY the one matching LLM_PROVIDER
#
# LLM_PROVIDER itself lives in .env.defaults; it is not a secret.
#
# No per-provider install step: all four SDKs are core dependencies in
# pyproject.toml, so a plain `uv sync` locally and Vercel's own build (it reads
# pyproject.toml + uv.lock) both have every one of them. Switching provider is
# one environment variable, never a reinstall or a redeploy config change.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- LLM_PROVIDER="bedrock" ---
# Bedrock authenticates with AWS credentials, not an API key.
#
# LOCAL DEVELOPMENT: leave AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID / AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY COMMENTED
# OUT. boto3 reads environment variables BEFORE ~/.aws/credentials, so setting
# them here -- even to a placeholder like "..." -- shadows your working local
# profile and Bedrock fails with a confusing "UnrecognizedClientException:
# The security token included in the request is invalid".
#
# DEPLOYMENT (Vercel): serverless has no ~/.aws, so set both as real
# environment variables in the Vercel dashboard instead of in this file.
#AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID="..."
#AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY="..."

# --- LLM_PROVIDER="anthropic" --- Claude API key, from console.anthropic.com
#ANTHROPIC_API_KEY="sk-ant-..."

# --- LLM_PROVIDER="openai" --- OpenAI API key, from platform.openai.com  [DEFAULT]
#OPENAI_API_KEY="sk-..."

# --- LLM_PROVIDER="google" --- Gemini API key, from aistudio.google.com
#GOOGLE_API_KEY="..."


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database -- REQUIRED, including for local development
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The agent reads the same NeonDB Postgres instance everywhere; it no longer
# falls back to SQLite on a laptop.
#
# That used to be the arrangement, and it produced a specific bug: every SQL
# example in the system prompt was written and tested against SQLite, then
# shipped to a Postgres deployment where strftime, JULIANDAY and
# `DATE(x, '-30 days')` do not exist. Nothing crashed, because the agent's SQL
# tool hands database errors back to the model as text, so the only symptom was
# worse answers in production. One database everywhere is what keeps a single
# prompt honest.
#
# SQLite is still the seed the dataset ships (data/data.sqlite.example) and the
# source the sync reads. First-time setup:
#
#     mise run setup-db     # seed tmp/data.sqlite from the committed copy
#     mise run sync-db      # publish it into this project's Postgres schema
#
# Use the -pooler host. Tables live in the schema named by SCHEMA_NAME in
# .env.defaults, so other projects can share this instance untouched.
#
# DB_PORT is in .env.defaults -- it is 5432 and identifies nothing.
#DB_HOST="...-pooler.c-4.us-east-1.aws.neon.tech"
#DB_USER="neondb_owner"
#DB_PASS="npg_1a2b3c..."
#DB_NAME="neondb"


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Monthly LLM spend cap -- the one secret-ish part
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The on/off flag and the caps are in .env.defaults. Only the table name is
# here, because it points at a real resource in your AWS account.
#
# The table's partition key must be `app_id` (type String); nothing else about
# it matters. Row keys look like
# `learn-bi-agent-app-scaffold:<SCHEMA_NAME>:YYYY-MM`, so one table can hold the
# counters for many deployments.
#DYNAMODB_TABLE_NAME_QUOTA="my-llm-quota"
